const e=[{id:"pe-l-civil-start",moduleId:"m11-civil-procedure",title:"How a court case actually starts",minutes:20,summary:"Nobody just takes someone to court. There is a letter, a document, a fee, a delivery and a deadline, and this lesson walks through all of them.",plants:["pe-c-cp-01","pe-c-cp-02","pe-c-cp-03","pe-c-cp-04"],prepares:["pe-p-civil-01"],reading:[{bookId:"janab-civil-procedure",where:"the chapters on commencement of proceedings and on service"}],source:"Rules of Court 2012; Limitation Act 1953; Subordinate Courts Act 1948.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Check the current Rules of Court 2012 at lom.agc.gov.my for the modes of commencement, the time to enter appearance and the time to file a defence. The monetary limits of the subordinate courts are deliberately not stated here; look them up before filing.",plate:"letter-of-demand",plateCaption:"Before any court: a letter and a deadline. Most disputes that end well end here.",readMinutes:6,sections:[{id:"sec-pe-l-civil-start-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Owed a RM1,500 deposit and threatening court — what does that actually involve?",h:"Nobody just takes someone to court",body:[{t:"p",text:"Nurul is owed her RM1,500 rental deposit. She tells her landlord she will take him to court. What does that actually involve?"},{t:"p",text:"A letter. A document. A fee at a counter. A delivery. Then a clock starts running for the other side."},{t:"p",text:"Almost all of a civil case is paperwork and dates. That surprises people, and it should change how you picture a lawsuit."},{t:"diagram",kind:"flow",title:"What taking someone to court actually involves",caption:"Almost all of a civil case is paperwork and dates. That surprises people, and it should change how you picture a lawsuit.",alt:"Six steps. A letter of demand, which is not compulsory and is cheap. Check the limitation clock: six years, running from when the right to sue arose. Choose the court and the document, which is decided by what you claim. File and pay the fee at the counter. Serve it, and they must actually receive it. Then their clock starts, with a deadline to answer.",steps:[{label:"Letter of demand",note:"not compulsory, and cheap"},{label:"Check the clock",note:"six years, from when the right arose"},{label:"Court and document",note:"what you claim decides where"},{label:"File and pay",note:"the fee at the counter"},{label:"Serve it",note:"they must actually receive it"},{label:"Their clock starts",note:"a deadline to answer"}]}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-civil-start-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Send the letter, and watch the clock: six years for contract and tort, from accrual.",h:"Before the court: the letter, and the clock",body:[{t:"p",text:"The usual first step is a letter of demand. It is a written request to pay by a stated date. It is not compulsory and it is cheap."},{t:"p",text:"It often works, and it costs a claimant nothing to be able to show the court that they asked first."},{t:"p",text:"Then check the clock, because a claim brought too late is dead however good it is."},{t:"rule",text:"Actions founded on contract or on tort must generally be brought within six years from the date on which the cause of action accrued.",source:"Limitation Act 1953"},{t:"caution",text:"The six years run from when the right to sue arose, not from when you found out or when you got angry. In contract that is the breach. In tort it is usually when the damage happened."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-civil-start-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The courts are tiered by the size of the claim, and the document depends on the kind of dispute.",h:"Which court, and which document",body:[{t:"p",text:"Malaysia's civil courts are tiered by the size of the claim. The Magistrates' Court takes the smallest cases, the Sessions Court the middle range, the High Court the rest."},{t:"caution",text:"The money limits sit in the Subordinate Courts Act 1948 and have been raised over the years. Look up the current figures before filing. Do not trust a number from an old textbook or a blog."},{t:"p",text:"Then pick the document that starts the case. Under the Rules of Court 2012 there are two main ones."},{t:"list",items:["A **writ**, where the fight is about what happened. Witnesses come and are questioned by the other side.","An **originating summons**, where the facts are agreed and the fight is about what a document means."]},{t:"example",text:"Arguing over whether the landlord promised to return the whole deposit is a writ. Arguing over what clause 8 of a signed tenancy agreement means is an originating summons."},{t:"p",text:"The person suing is the **plaintiff**. The person sued is the **defendant**."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-civil-start-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Filing is not enough; the defendant must be served, and service must be proved.",h:"Filing, and then service",body:[{t:"p",text:"Filing means lodging the document with the court and paying the fee. The case now has a number and exists."},{t:"p",text:"Service means getting that document into the defendant's hands, in the manner the rules require. It is a formal step with its own proof."},{t:"rule",text:"Proceedings must be served on the defendant in the manner prescribed by the Rules of Court 2012, and service must be proved to the court.",source:"Rules of Court 2012"},{t:"caution",text:"People underrate service constantly. A defendant who was never properly served can have a judgment set aside months later, on that ground alone."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-civil-start-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Once served, the defendant has a short fixed period to enter an appearance.",h:"The defendant's clock starts",body:[{t:"p",text:"Once served, the defendant has a short fixed period to enter an **appearance**. That is a filed notice saying they intend to contest the claim."},{t:"p",text:"Then a further period to file a **defence**, which is the document answering the claim point by point."},{t:"p",text:"Miss either and the plaintiff may ask for **judgment in default**. That is judgment without a trial, because nobody turned up to argue."},{t:"caution",text:"A default judgment can often be set aside, but not for free and not as of right. The deadlines are the real subject matter of this module."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-civil-start-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A case number, two documents, and a defendant who knows — that is all 'starting a case' means.",h:"What you have at the end of all that",body:[{t:"p",text:"A case number, two documents, and a defendant who knows he is being sued. That is the whole of 'starting a case'."},{t:"p",text:"Nothing has been decided yet. What happens next is the next lesson."}]}],quiz:[{id:"pe-q-cvs-1",q:"A tenancy dispute is about whether the landlord promised to return the whole deposit. He denies saying it. Which document starts the case?",options:["An originating summons","A writ","Either, they are interchangeable","A letter of demand"],answer:1,why:"The parties disagree about what happened, so witnesses are needed and a writ is the right mode. An originating summons is for agreed facts and a question of construction.",source:"Rules of Court 2012, Order 5"},{id:"pe-q-cvs-2",q:"When does the six-year limitation period for a contract claim start running?",options:["When the claimant found out about the problem","When the claimant first asked for payment","When the cause of action accrued, which in contract is the breach","When the case is filed in court"],answer:2,why:"Accrual is not discovery and not anger. Confusing accrual with the day you noticed is how a good claim gets thrown out.",source:"Limitation Act 1953 s 6; in contract the cause of action accrues on breach"},{id:"pe-q-cvs-3",q:"Why is service treated as a formal step rather than a formality?",options:["The court fee is only payable on service","It fixes the limitation date","It decides which court hears the case","A judgment can be set aside later if service was defective"],answer:3,why:"Proper service is what makes the proceedings binding on a defendant. Sloppy service is one of the commonest ways a plaintiff loses ground they had already won.",source:"Rules of Court 2012; the provisions on service of originating process"},{id:"pe-q-cvs-4",q:"The defendant is served and then does nothing at all. What can the plaintiff seek?",options:["An immediate trial date","A striking out of the defence","Nothing, until the defendant appears","Judgment in default"],answer:3,why:"Missing the deadline to enter appearance or file a defence exposes a defendant to judgment without a trial. It can often be set aside, but not as of right.",source:"Rules of Court 2012, Order 13"},{id:"pe-q-cvs-5",q:"You want to know the money limit of the Sessions Court. What should you do?",options:["Use the figure in the most popular textbook","Ask the court clerk and rely on that","Look up the current figure in the Subordinate Courts Act 1948","Assume it is the same as the High Court"],answer:2,why:"The limits have been raised over the years, so older sources are wrong rather than merely dated. Checking the statute takes a minute and filing in the wrong court wastes months.",source:"Subordinate Courts Act 1948; the limits are statutory figures, raised from time to time"}]},{id:"pe-l-civil-journey",moduleId:"m11-civil-procedure",title:"From papers to judgment, and then to getting paid",minutes:22,summary:"Pleadings, discovery, the applications that kill cases early, the trial, and the fact that winning is not the same as being paid.",plants:["pe-c-cp-05","pe-c-cp-06","pe-c-cp-07","pe-c-cp-08"],prepares:["pe-p-civil-01"],reading:[{bookId:"janab-civil-procedure",where:"the chapters on pleadings, discovery, summary judgment and enforcement"}],source:"Rules of Court 2012, including Order 14 and Order 18 rule 19; ordinary practice on enforcement and appeals.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Check the current Rules of Court 2012 at lom.agc.gov.my. Order 14 and Order 18 rule 19 are named; the discovery, case management and enforcement provisions are described generally and should be read in the Rules themselves. Appeal periods come from the Courts of Judicature Act 1964 and the appellate rules.",plate:"road-to-judgment",plateCaption:"Papers to judgment, and the further road from judgment to being paid — the stretch an advice that stops at “you will win” leaves out.",readMinutes:8,sections:[{id:"sec-pe-l-civil-journey-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Most civil claims never reach trial: they end on a missed deadline or a short application.",h:"Where cases are really decided",body:[{t:"p",text:"Most civil claims never reach a trial. They end on a missed deadline, a badly drafted document, or an application heard in twenty minutes."},{t:"p",text:"Films get this wrong. The courtroom speech is the rarest part of a case, and often the least important."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-civil-journey-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A pleading states the material facts, not the evidence for them and not the law.",h:"Pleadings: the fence around the argument",body:[{t:"p",text:"The statement of claim sets out the facts the plaintiff relies on. The defence answers them, point by point. Together these are the **pleadings**."},{t:"rule",text:"A pleading states the material facts relied on, not the evidence for them and not the law.",source:"Rules of Court 2012"},{t:"p",text:"Their job is to fix what the case is about, so that neither side is ambushed on the day."},{t:"caution",text:"You are bound by your pleadings. A case you did not plead generally cannot be run at trial, however true it is. And an allegation the defence does not answer is usually treated as admitted."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-civil-journey-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Each side must disclose the relevant documents it holds, including the ones that hurt.",h:"Discovery: showing the other side your papers",body:[{t:"p",text:"Each side must disclose the documents it holds that are relevant to the case. Including the ones that hurt."},{t:"example",text:"A landlord must disclose his own repair invoices, even though they show he charged two tenants for the same broken door."},{t:"p",text:"This stage is called **discovery**, or disclosure. Non-lawyers find it the most surprising rule in the whole system."},{t:"caution",text:"It is not unlimited. Some documents are privileged, such as legal advice from your own lawyer. Those are listed but not handed over."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-civil-journey-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Default judgment, summary judgment, striking out — the applications that end cases early.",h:"The applications that end cases early",body:[{t:"list",items:["**Judgment in default**, where the defendant simply missed a deadline.","**Summary judgment** under Order 14, where the plaintiff says the defence has no substance and there is nothing worth trying.","**Striking out** under Order 18 rule 19, where a pleading is so bad on its face that it discloses no case at all."]},{t:"caution",text:"The last two ask different questions. Striking out looks at the document and assumes the pleaded facts are true. Summary judgment looks at the evidence and asks whether there is a triable issue."},{t:"p",text:"The threshold for both is deliberately high. The court is taking a trial away from a party, and it does not do that lightly."},{t:"compare",title:"Two applications, two different questions",left:{h:"Striking out — O 18 r 19",items:["Looks at the document.","Assumes the pleaded facts are true.","Asks whether the pleading discloses any reasonable cause of action at all.","The complaint is that it is bad on its face."]},right:{h:"Summary judgment — O 14",items:["Looks at the evidence.","Asks whether there is a real defence fit to be tried.","The facts are not assumed; they are examined on affidavit.","The complaint is that the defence has no substance."]}}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-civil-journey-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Case management fixes dates and pushes both sides along before any trial.",h:"Case management, then trial",body:[{t:"p",text:"Before trial the court holds case management hearings. It fixes dates, checks that documents have been exchanged, and pushes both sides along."},{t:"p",text:"At trial the witnesses give evidence and are cross-examined. The judge delivers judgment, sometimes that day, often months later."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-civil-journey-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Winning gives you a piece of paper saying you are owed something.",h:"A judgment is not money",body:[{t:"predict",prompt:"Nurul wins. The court orders the landlord to pay her RM1,500, and he does nothing at all. What happens next?",options:["The court collects the money and passes it to her","He must pay, or go to prison","Nothing by itself. Getting the money is a separate job, and it is hers"],answer:2,reveal:"The third. It surprises nearly everybody. The judgment is a piece of paper saying the debt exists. Turning it into ringgit is a separate job, with its own fees, and the court does not start it for you. Assuming a win ends the matter is sensible, and it is what this section corrects."},{t:"p",text:"Here is the part nobody expects. Winning gives you a piece of paper saying you are owed something."},{t:"p",text:"Turning that paper into ringgit is a separate exercise called **enforcement**, with its own procedures and its own costs."},{t:"list",items:["Seizure and sale of the debtor's goods.","Garnishee proceedings, which capture money a third party owes the debtor, such as a bank balance.","A charge over land the debtor owns.","Winding up a company, or bankruptcy proceedings against a person."]},{t:"caution",text:"A judgment against someone with nothing is worth nothing. Ask what a defendant can actually pay before you spend two years suing them."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-civil-journey-6",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Appeals run upward, sometimes needing permission, and carry the shortest deadline in the case.",h:"Appeals, and the shortest deadline in the case",body:[{t:"p",text:"A losing party may appeal upward through the court hierarchy. At some levels the court's permission is needed first."},{t:"caution",text:"The time to file a notice of appeal is short and it is applied strictly. Missing it usually ends the matter, whatever the merits were."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-civil-journey-7",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Demand, file, serve, plead, disclose, manage, try, judge, enforce, appeal.",h:"The shape to remember",body:[{t:"p",text:"Demand, file, serve, plead, disclose, manage, try, judge, enforce, appeal."},{t:"diagram",kind:"flow",title:"The whole shape, end to end",caption:"Every one of these carries a deadline. That, rather than eloquence, is what a litigation practice mostly consists of.",alt:"Ten stages, each carrying a deadline. Demand, a letter before action. File, by writ or originating summons. Serve, and they must actually get it. Plead, which fences the argument. Disclose, including what hurts you. Manage, where the court fixes the dates. Try, with witnesses cross-examined. Judge, that day or months later. Enforce, because a judgment is not money. Appeal, on the shortest deadline of all.",steps:[{label:"Demand",note:"a letter before action"},{label:"File",note:"writ, or originating summons"},{label:"Serve",note:"they must actually get it"},{label:"Plead",note:"the fence around the argument"},{label:"Disclose",note:"including what hurts you"},{label:"Manage",note:"the court fixes the dates"},{label:"Try",note:"witnesses, cross-examined"},{label:"Judge",note:"that day, or months later"},{label:"Enforce",note:"a judgment is not money"},{label:"Appeal",note:"the shortest deadline of all"}]},{t:"p",text:"Every one of those steps carries a deadline. That, rather than eloquence, is what a litigation practice mostly consists of."}]}],quiz:[{id:"pe-q-cvj-1",q:"What belongs in a pleading?",options:["The evidence that proves each fact","The material facts relied on","The legal argument in full","The witness statements"],answer:1,why:"Pleadings fix what the case is about, in facts. Loading them with evidence or argument is a standard drafting error and invites an application to strike out.",source:"Rules of Court 2012, Order 18"},{id:"pe-q-cvj-2",q:"During discovery, must a party disclose a document that damages its own case?",options:["No, a party never has to help its opponent","Only if the other side already knows it exists","Only if the court orders it specifically","Yes, if it is relevant and not privileged"],answer:3,why:"Relevance, not helpfulness, decides disclosure. The exception is privilege, and privileged documents are listed rather than handed over.",source:"Rules of Court 2012, Order 24"},{id:"pe-q-cvj-3",q:"Which application assumes the pleaded facts are true and asks whether they disclose a case?",options:["Striking out","Summary judgment","Judgment in default","An interim injunction"],answer:0,why:"Striking out attacks the pleading as a document. Summary judgment looks at the evidence and asks whether there is a triable issue, which is a different test.",source:"Rules of Court 2012, Order 18 rule 19"},{id:"pe-q-cvj-4",q:"You win RM20,000 in court. Why might you still receive nothing?",options:["The judgment expires after one year","The court keeps the money until an appeal is over","Costs always cancel out the award","A judgment must be enforced, and enforcement needs assets to reach"],answer:3,why:"A judgment is a declaration of debt, not a payment. If the defendant has no goods, land or bank balance, there is nothing for enforcement to bite on.",source:"Rules of Court 2012; enforcement is a separate exercise from obtaining judgment"},{id:"pe-q-cvj-5",q:"Your client lost, and strongly wants to appeal. What is the first thing to check?",options:["Whether a different judge would have decided differently","How much the appeal will cost","Whether new evidence has emerged","The time limit for filing the notice of appeal"],answer:3,why:"Appeal periods are short and strictly applied, so the deadline governs everything else. Merits are worth nothing once the time has gone.",source:"Standard appellate practice; the time for a notice of appeal is fixed by the rules and strictly applied"}]},{id:"l-civil-procedure",moduleId:"m11-civil-procedure",title:"How a civil action is shaped",minutes:30,summary:"Choosing how to start an action, setting out a case in a pleading, and the interlocutory steps that dispose of most disputes before trial.",plants:[],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"janab-civil-procedure",where:"the chapters on commencement, pleadings and interlocutory applications"},{bookId:"malaysian-civil-procedure-white-book",where:"as the reference — the Rules with commentary, order by order"}],source:"Rules of Court 2012, Orders 5, 13, 14, 18, 24; Limitation Act 1953; Courts of Judicature Act 1964.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Order numbers here are given as orientation and should be confirmed against the current Rules of Court 2012 before being cited. Limitation periods have exceptions — disability, fraud, concealment, and the position on latent damage — which are not covered above and must be read in the Limitation Act 1953 itself.",plate:"files",plateCaption:"Bundles on a registry shelf. Most of them end on an interlocutory application rather than at a trial.",readMinutes:9,sections:[{id:"sec-l-civil-procedure-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"RM18,000 of unpaid work and a man who wants his day in court — which he may never get.",h:"The case that never reaches trial",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Rahim did RM18,000 of renovation work on a shophouse and was never paid. He wants his day in court. He may well never get one."},{t:"p",text:"Most civil disputes are never tried. They are decided by a limitation point, a striking out, an application for summary judgment, or a settlement that one of those made likely."},{t:"p",text:"Procedure is where the outcome is usually decided, so treating it as the boring administrative part is a mistake people make exactly once."},{t:"p",text:"The Rules of Court 2012 govern, and they brought together what used to be separate sets of rules for the High Court and the subordinate courts."},{t:"diagram",kind:"flow",title:"Where a civil action can stop",caption:"Only the last box is a trial. Each of the earlier ones ends cases outright, which is why procedure decides more outcomes than the substantive law does.",alt:"Five stages, each of which can end the action. Limitation: out of time ends it here. Mode: writ, or originating summons. Pleadings, which fix what the trial can be about. Applications: striking out, and summary judgment. Then trial, reached by a minority of disputes.",steps:[{label:"Limitation",note:"out of time ends it here"},{label:"Mode",note:"writ, or originating summons"},{label:"Pleadings",note:"they fix what the trial can be about"},{label:"Applications",note:"striking out, summary judgment"},{label:"Trial",note:"reached by a minority of disputes"}]}]},{id:"sec-l-civil-procedure-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Six years for contract and tort, twelve to recover land, running from accrual rather than from the wrong.",h:"First: is it already too late?",body:[{t:"p",text:"The first question on any set of facts is whether the claim is already out of time. If it is, nothing else about the case matters at all."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Encik Rahim finished the renovation seven years ago and was never paid. Before you read on — what happens to his claim now?",options:["He is out of time, and the court will not hear it","He can still sue — a debt does not stop being owed","It depends on when he last chased the shophouse owner for the money"],answer:0,reveal:"Out of time. If you chose the first, you are not wrong about the money. The debt is still owed and it never stops being owed. What runs out is the court. A contract claim has to be brought within six years, counted from the breach, and chasing somebody for payment does not restart that clock. It is the first thing to check on any facts, because nothing else matters if the answer is no."},{t:"rule",text:"Actions founded on contract or on tort must generally be brought within six years of the date on which the cause of action accrued; actions to recover land, within twelve.",source:"Limitation Act 1953"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: six years for a contract or tort claim, twelve to recover land, counted from the moment the claim arose."},{t:"caution",text:"Accrual is not the same thing as the wrongful act. In contract the cause of action accrues on breach. In tort it generally accrues when damage occurs, which may be a good deal later. Getting accrual wrong is how a claim ends up filed a year out of time."}]},{id:"sec-l-civil-procedure-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Writ where the facts are disputed; originating summons where the question is one of construction.",h:"How to start",body:[{t:"p",text:"There are two main ways to begin an action, and the choice between them is not a matter of taste."},{t:"list",items:["**Writ** — the mode where there is likely to be a real dispute about the facts. Evidence is given aloud in court and tested by cross-examination.","**Originating summons** — the mode for a question of law on undisputed facts, or about what a document or statute means. Evidence goes in by affidavit, which is a written statement sworn to be true."]},{t:"example",text:"A dispute about what a clause in a lease means, where nobody disagrees about what was signed, is an originating summons. A dispute about whether a promise was made at a meeting is a writ."},{t:"p",text:"Choosing wrongly is not usually fatal, because the court can order the proceedings to carry on as if they had begun the other way. But it wastes time and it costs money."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"Which mode, and why it is not a matter of taste",caption:"Choosing wrongly is rarely fatal: the court can order the action to continue as if it had begun the other way. But it costs time the choice never needed to cost.",alt:"A question — is the real dispute about the facts? — branching two ways. Yes: issue a writ, where evidence is given aloud and tested by cross-examination. No, it is a question of law on undisputed facts or about the meaning of a document: an originating summons, decided on affidavit.",question:"Is the real dispute about the facts?",branches:[{cond:"yes",label:"Writ",note:"witnesses, cross-examined"},{cond:"no — law on agreed facts",label:"Originating summons",note:"decided on affidavit"}]}]},{id:"sec-l-civil-procedure-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Plead material facts, not the evidence and not the law.",h:"Pleadings",body:[{t:"p",text:"**Pleadings** are the documents setting out each side's case in material facts. They are what define the issues the trial will be about."},{t:"p",text:"The statement of claim sets out the material facts the plaintiff relies on. The defence answers them, and a reply may follow after that."},{t:"rule",text:"Plead material facts, not evidence and not law. A pleading states what happened, in the facts that give rise to the right claimed.",source:"Rules of Court 2012, Order 18"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: say what happened. Do not say how you will prove it, and do not argue the law."},{t:"p",text:"Two consequences follow, and both of them bite. A party is bound by their pleadings, and cannot run a case at trial that was never pleaded."},{t:"p",text:"And an allegation that is not answered in the defence is generally taken as admitted. So a bare denial of everything is not a defence, and a silence is an admission."},{t:"caution",text:"Some matters must be pleaded specifically and with particulars: fraud, misrepresentation, breach of trust, and anything else that would take the other side by surprise. Pleading fraud without particulars is worse than not pleading it at all."}]},{id:"sec-l-civil-procedure-4",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Default judgment, summary judgment, striking out and discovery are where most cases actually end.",h:"The applications that end cases",body:[{t:"list",items:["Judgment in default, where the defendant fails to enter an appearance or to serve a defence in time.","Summary judgment, where there is no defence with a real prospect of success and no other reason for a trial.","Striking out, where a pleading discloses no reasonable cause of action or defence, is scandalous or vexatious, or is an abuse of process.","Discovery, where each side must disclose the documents relevant to the matters in question, including the documents that hurt them."]},{t:"p",text:"Summary judgment and striking out decide the most cases, and the threshold for both of them is deliberately high. The court is taking a trial away from a party, so the applicant must show the case is clear, and any genuinely triable issue defeats the application."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not confuse the two. Striking out attacks the pleading as a document: assume every pleaded fact is true, and ask whether together they disclose a case. Summary judgment looks at the evidence and asks whether the defence is real."},{t:"example",text:"Rahim's claim is properly pleaded, so an application to strike it out would fail. But if the only answer to it is a bare denial, with no invoice, no witness and no explanation, summary judgment may well succeed."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A statement of claim is carefully drafted, but even if every word of it were true it would still not be a claim the law recognises. Which application fits?",options:["Striking out — the pleading discloses no reasonable cause of action","Summary judgment — there is no defence with a real prospect of success","Judgment in default — the claim is a bad one"],answer:0,why:"Striking out attacks the pleading as a document. You assume every pleaded fact is true and ask whether together they add up to a case, which is exactly what fails here. Summary judgment looks at the evidence, and the evidence is not the problem. Default judgment has nothing to do with the merits at all. It is for a defendant who missed a deadline."}]},{id:"sec-l-civil-procedure-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A judgment is a piece of paper; enforcement is a separate exercise.",h:"Winning is not the same as being paid",body:[{t:"p",text:"A judgment is a piece of paper rather than money. Enforcement is a separate exercise with procedures of its own."},{t:"p",text:"They include execution against goods, garnishment of debts owed to the judgment debtor, charging orders over land, and winding up or bankruptcy where those fit. **Garnishment** means intercepting money that somebody else owes to the debtor."},{t:"p",text:"Telling a client that they have won is incomplete advice if the defendant has nothing to take."},{t:"p",text:"Appeals run up through the hierarchy under the Courts of Judicature Act 1964, with leave required at certain stages. The time limits for appealing are short and they are applied strictly."}]},{id:"sec-l-civil-procedure-6",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Take a simple set of facts and write out the sequence from letter to payment.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"There are no flashcards or problems for this module yet. The exercise that teaches it fastest is to take a simple set of facts and write out the sequence."},{t:"p",text:"Limitation, then mode of commencement, then the pleading, then the application the other side would make, and finally what you would need to defeat it."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qcvp1",q:"When does a contract cause of action accrue for limitation purposes?",options:["When the loss is suffered","When the claimant finds out","When payment is demanded","On breach"],answer:3,why:"In tort it generally accrues when damage occurs, which can be much later than the act.",source:"Limitation Act 1953 s 6; in contract the cause of action accrues on breach"},{id:"qcvp2",q:"When is a writ the right way to begin?",options:["Where there is likely to be a real dispute about the facts","Where the dispute is about what a document means","Where the facts are agreed","Where urgent relief is needed"],answer:0,why:"An originating summons suits a question of law, or of construction, on undisputed facts.",source:"Rules of Court 2012, Order 5"},{id:"qcvp3",q:"What must a pleading contain?",options:["The evidence relied on","The legal argument","The witness statements","Material facts, not evidence and not law"],answer:3,why:"And some matters — fraud, misrepresentation, breach of trust — must be pleaded specifically, with particulars.",source:"Rules of Court 2012, Order 18"},{id:"qcvp4",q:"What is the difference between striking out and summary judgment?",options:["They are two names for the same application","Striking out is available to defendants only","Striking out attacks the pleading as a document; summary judgment looks at the evidence","Summary judgment is available only in the High Court"],answer:2,why:"On a strike-out for no reasonable cause of action you assume every pleaded fact is true.",source:"Rules of Court 2012, Order 14 and Order 18 rule 19"},{id:"qcvp5",q:"What is the general limitation period for an action to recover land?",options:["Six years","Twelve years","Three years","Twenty years"],answer:1,why:"Contract and tort are generally six. Land is twelve.",source:"Limitation Act 1953; twelve years for an action to recover land"}]},{id:"l-service-of-process",moduleId:"m11-civil-procedure",title:"Getting the papers into the defendant's hands",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Filing a claim opens a file, but it does not bind anybody. Service is the step that does, and this lesson covers how it is done, how it is proved, and what to do when the defendant cannot be found.",source:"Rules of Court 2012 — the provisions on service of originating process, on substituted service, and on service out of the jurisdiction.",verify:"This lesson states no Order or rule number for service on purpose; confirm it in the current Rules of Court 2012 and record the date you checked. The same goes for substituted service and for service out of the jurisdiction. The permitted modes, and the time a served defendant has to respond, are both amended from time to time.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"rules-of-court-2012",where:"the orders on service of originating process and on service out of the jurisdiction — read the permitted modes and the proof required"},{bookId:"janab-civil-procedure",where:"the chapter on service, and the worked examples on substituted service"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-service-of-process-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Filing opens a file. Service is the step that binds a defendant to it.",h:"The defendant who moved house",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Mariam Zakaria supplies steel in Ipoh. Encik Danial owes her RM42,000 for three deliveries. She files a writ, pays the fee, and goes home satisfied."},{t:"p",text:"Nothing has happened to Danial yet. The court has a file. He does not know it exists, and he is not bound by anything in it."},{t:"p",text:"Service is the step that changes that. It is how the papers reach the defendant, in a way the court will accept as proof."},{t:"rule",text:"Originating process must be served on every defendant in a manner the Rules of Court 2012 permit, and service must be proved to the court before the court will act on a defendant's failure to respond.",source:"Rules of Court 2012"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: filing opens a file, and service starts the defendant's clock. Until then the case is a document sitting in a registry."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Mariam's writ is filed and sealed. It is still in her lawyer's drawer a month later. What can she do about Danial's silence?",options:["Nothing yet. His time to answer has not started, because he has not been served","Ask for judgment in default, since he has not answered","Ask the court to serve it for her, since filing is complete"],answer:0,reveal:"Nothing yet. The clock a defendant misses is the one that starts on service, not on filing. A default judgment against an unserved defendant is worth very little. He can come back and have it set aside, and the costs of that will usually be hers."}]},{id:"sec-l-service-of-process-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"An individual, a company and a defendant with solicitors on the record are each reached differently.",h:"Who you are serving changes the route",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Rules set out the permitted modes of service. Which one fits depends on what kind of defendant you have."},{t:"p",text:"Personal service means handing the document to the defendant himself. It is the safest route, and for some documents it is the required one."},{t:"table",caption:"Who the defendant is, and what that changes",columns:["The defendant","The usual route","What catches people out"],rows:[["An individual","Personal service, or another mode the Rules allow","He must actually receive it. A document left at an old address proves nothing."],["A company","Service at its registered office","The registered office is often an accountant's address rather than where the company trades."],["A defendant with solicitors","Service on the solicitors, where they have agreed to accept it","The agreement should be in writing. An oral assurance is not proof."],["A defendant outside Malaysia","Service abroad, with the court's leave","Leave is granted only in the categories the Rules set out, and the exercise is slow."]],alt:"Four kinds of defendant and how each is reached. An individual is served personally or by another permitted mode, and must actually receive it. A company is served at its registered office, which is often an accountant's address rather than where it trades. A defendant with solicitors may be served on them where they have agreed in writing to accept service. A defendant outside Malaysia is served abroad with the court's leave, granted only in the categories the Rules set out."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not assume the address on a letterhead is good for service. Check the register for a company, and check the Rules for the mode, before you send anything."}]},{id:"sec-l-service-of-process-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Substituted service needs an order first, granted on evidence of the attempts that failed.",h:"When you cannot find him",body:[{t:"p",text:"Mariam's process server attends Danial's house three times. The gate is locked each time. A neighbour says he moved to Penang months ago."},{t:"p",text:"This is common, and the Rules have an answer for it. It is called substituted service."},{t:"p",text:"Substituted service is service by some other means, ordered by the court, where the ordinary means cannot practicably be used."},{t:"rule",text:"Where personal service cannot practicably be effected, the court may order that service be effected by such other means as it thinks fit, on evidence of the attempts already made and of the likelihood that the substituted means will bring the document to the defendant's notice.",source:"Rules of Court 2012"},{t:"p",text:"Two things are being asked for. First, proof that you genuinely tried. Second, a method that is actually likely to reach him."},{t:"example",text:"The affidavit sets out three attendances, with dates, times and what the process server saw. The order sought is for the papers to be pasted on the gate and advertised once in a newspaper he is likely to read."},{t:"caution",text:"You need the order before you serve, not after. Pasting a writ on a gate without an order is not service, and everything built on it can fall."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A process server calls once, at 11am on a working day, finds nobody in, and applies for substituted service. What is the likely response?",options:["The order will be granted, because one attempt shows service is impracticable","The court will want better evidence that personal service cannot practicably be effected","The application will be refused, because substituted service is only for defendants abroad"],answer:1,why:"One visit in office hours shows very little. The court is being asked to accept something less than personal service, so it wants evidence of real attempts. Substituted service is not limited to defendants abroad. It is for defendants who cannot practicably be served in the ordinary way."}]},{id:"sec-l-service-of-process-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A defendant never properly served can return months later and have the judgment set aside.",h:"Service that fails, and what follows",body:[{t:"p",text:"Bad service is not a technicality. It is one of the grounds on which finished judgments are undone."},{t:"list",items:["Keep the affidavit of service. It is the proof, and without it the court has only your word.","An irregularity in service can often be cured or waived. A fundamental defect may make the judgment a nullity instead.","A defendant who wants a judgment set aside must usually move promptly, and delay counts against him.","A defendant who takes a step in the action knowing of the defect may be treated as having accepted service."]},{t:"p",text:"So the question at the start is not only whether you can win. It is whether you can prove the defendant knew he was being sued."},{t:"caution",text:"Service abroad is a separate exercise and needs the court's leave. Treaty arrangements and the law of the receiving country both matter, and it is slow. Build the delay into your timetable."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-discovery-and-privilege",moduleId:"m11-civil-procedure",title:"Discovery, privilege, and the document you wish you had not written",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Each side must disclose the relevant documents it holds, including the ones that damage its own case. This lesson covers what relevance means, what privilege really protects, and what to do when the other side holds back.",source:"Rules of Court 2012 — the provisions on discovery and inspection, on interrogatories, and on production by a non-party. Order 18 for pleadings. Evidence Act 1950 on privileged professional communications.",verify:"This lesson states no Order or rule number for discovery on purpose; confirm it in the current Rules of Court 2012 and record the date you checked. The same goes for inspection, interrogatories and subpoenas. Order 18 is named here only for pleadings. Privilege is governed partly by the Evidence Act 1950 as well as by the Rules, and the two must be read together.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"rules-of-court-2012",where:"the orders on discovery and inspection of documents, and on interrogatories"},{bookId:"malaysian-civil-procedure-white-book",where:"the discovery orders with commentary, and the notes on claims to privilege"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-discovery-and-privilege-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"You disclose a relevant document because it is relevant, not because it helps you.",h:"The site diary that ruins your own case",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Kumaran runs a contracting firm in Shah Alam. He is suing a developer for RM380,000 in unpaid progress claims."},{t:"p",text:"His own site diary records two weeks when his crew never turned up. He asks whether he has to show it to the other side."},{t:"p",text:"He does. This is the rule that surprises non-lawyers more than any other in civil procedure."},{t:"rule",text:"Each party must disclose the documents in its possession, custody or power which are relevant to the matters in question in the action, whether those documents support that party's case or damage it. Disclosure is made by list, and the other party may then inspect the listed documents which are not privileged.",source:"Rules of Court 2012"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: you list what you hold, including what hurts. The other side may then ask to look at it."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Kumaran's lawyer suggests leaving the site diary off the list, since the developer does not know it exists. What is wrong with that?",options:["Nothing, so long as the diary is never referred to at trial","It breaks a duty owed to the court, and the consequences land on Kumaran as well as on his lawyer","Nothing, because a party never has to produce a document against itself"],answer:1,reveal:"The first. Disclosure is a duty owed to the court, not a tactic. A claim can be struck out for a deliberate failure, and a document that surfaces later does far more damage than one disclosed at the start. The instinct behind the other two answers is natural. It is exactly what this rule overrides."}]},{id:"sec-l-discovery-and-privilege-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Narrow pleadings narrow discovery, which is why drafting and disclosure are really one skill.",h:"Relevance is fixed by the pleadings",body:[{t:"p",text:"Relevant to what? To the matters in question in the action. And those are set by the pleadings."},{t:"rule",text:"A pleading states the material facts relied on, not the evidence for them and not the law. Because the pleadings define the issues in the action, they also fix the boundary of what is relevant, and therefore of what must be disclosed.",source:"Rules of Court 2012, Order 18"},{t:"p",text:"So a claim pleaded wide invites wide discovery. A claim pleaded tightly keeps the exercise proportionate."},{t:"p",text:"A document is not only paper. Emails, spreadsheets, messages and drafts are documents too, and so is the version you deleted but still hold in a backup."},{t:"caution",text:"Possession, custody or power is wider than possession. A file held by your accountant, or sitting on a server your company controls, may still be yours to produce."}]},{id:"sec-l-discovery-and-privilege-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Privilege is narrow, it belongs to the client, and confidentiality is not the same thing.",h:"Listed, but not handed over",body:[{t:"p",text:"Some documents are listed and then withheld from inspection. That is privilege, and it is narrower than most people expect."},{t:"list",items:["Legal advice privilege covers communications between a client and a lawyer for the purpose of giving or getting legal advice.","Litigation privilege covers documents created for the dominant purpose of litigation that is pending or reasonably anticipated.","Without prejudice communications are genuine settlement negotiations, and they are generally kept out on the question of who is right."]},{t:"table",caption:"Three documents people wrongly assume are protected",columns:["The document","Protected?","Why"],rows:[["Your solicitor's written advice on the claim","Yes","It is a communication made for the purpose of legal advice."],["The site diary, sent to your solicitor after the dispute began","No","It existed already. Sending a document to a lawyer does not clothe it in privilege."],["An internal email stamped strictly confidential","No","Confidentiality is not privilege, and a stamp does not create one."]],alt:"Three documents and whether privilege protects them. A solicitor's written advice on the claim is protected, because it is a communication made for the purpose of legal advice. A site diary that already existed and was later sent to the solicitor is not protected, because sending a document to a lawyer does not make it privileged. An internal email stamped strictly confidential is not protected either, because confidentiality is not privilege."},{t:"caution",text:"Privilege belongs to the client rather than the lawyer, and a client can lose it. Quoting the advice in order to justify your own conduct may waive it for the whole document."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A developer's insurer commissions an expert report after a wall collapses, at a point when a claim is clearly coming. Is the report likely to be protected?",options:["No, because expert reports are always disclosable","Probably, if the dominant purpose of creating it was the anticipated litigation","No, because it was commissioned by an insurer rather than by a lawyer"],answer:1,why:"Litigation privilege turns on the dominant purpose for which the document was created, not on who paid for it. A report made mainly to work out how to rebuild the wall would not be protected. Whether an expert report is later served for use at trial is a separate question from whether it must be disclosed now."}]},{id:"sec-l-discovery-and-privilege-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Name the document or the class you say is missing; a request that reads like a fishing trip is refused.",h:"When the other side holds back",body:[{t:"p",text:"Sometimes a list is obviously incomplete. There are ways to press, and they escalate."},{t:"list",items:["Apply for specific discovery, identifying the document or the class of documents you say has been left out.","Serve interrogatories, which are written questions the other side must answer on oath.","Ask the court to compel a person who is not a party to produce documents that person holds.","Ask for sanctions where an order is ignored, which can extend to striking out the claim or the defence."]},{t:"caution",text:"You must say what you are after and why you believe it exists. An application that asks to see everything and hopes something turns up will be refused as a fishing expedition."},{t:"p",text:"Keep the duty in mind after the list has been served. A relevant document that comes to light later still has to be disclosed."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-costs-in-civil-litigation",moduleId:"m11-civil-procedure",title:"Who pays for the fight",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Costs usually follow the event, but a winner rarely recovers what the fight actually cost. This lesson covers the two separate bills, what moves the figure, and how the risk of costs shapes what parties really do.",source:"Rules of Court 2012 — the provisions on costs, on taxation, and on security for costs. The ringgit figures in the worked example are illustrative only.",verify:"This lesson states no Order or rule number for costs on purpose; confirm it in the current Rules of Court 2012 and record the date you checked. The same goes for taxation and for security for costs. Scales and fixed costs are amended from time to time, so no scale figure is given here either.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"rules-of-court-2012",where:"the orders on costs and on security for costs — read the discretion, and the grounds on which it is exercised"},{bookId:"janab-civil-procedure",where:"the chapter on costs, and the section on taxation"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-costs-in-civil-litigation-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"What the loser pays is a contribution assessed by the court, not a refund of the winner's bill.",h:"Winning, and still being out of pocket",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Hasnah sues a renovation company over RM25,000 of defective work. After fourteen months, she wins."},{t:"p",text:"Her own solicitor's bill is RM19,000. The court orders the defendant to pay her costs of RM10,000. She has won, and she is out of pocket."},{t:"p",text:"This is normal rather than a mistake. Understanding why is the difference between advising a client and misleading one."},{t:"rule",text:"Costs are in the discretion of the court. The usual order is that costs follow the event, so that the unsuccessful party pays the successful party's costs; but the court may make a different order where the conduct of the parties, or the outcome on the individual issues, justifies it.",source:"Rules of Court 2012"},{t:"predict",prompt:"Hasnah asks why the defendant is not simply ordered to pay her RM19,000 bill. What is the answer?",options:["The costs a loser pays are a contribution assessed by the court, not the winner's actual bill","The court has no power to order costs above the amount claimed","She should have agreed a fixed figure with her solicitor before starting"],answer:0,reveal:"The second. There are two separate bills, and only one of them is the court's business. What her solicitor may charge her comes from her retainer with him. What the defendant must pay her is assessed by the court, and it is almost always the smaller number."}]},{id:"sec-l-costs-in-civil-litigation-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"One bill comes from your retainer with your own solicitor; the other is assessed by the court.",h:"Two bills, not one",body:[{t:"table",caption:"The two bills a winning party has to deal with",columns:["The bill","Who fixes it","What it covers"],rows:[["Solicitor and client","Your retainer with your own solicitor, subject to the court's supervision","Everything your solicitor did on your instructions"],["Party and party","The court, either by fixing a sum or on taxation","A contribution towards costs properly incurred in the action"]],alt:"Two bills. The solicitor and client bill is fixed by your retainer with your own solicitor, subject to the court's supervision. It covers everything the solicitor did on your instructions. The party and party bill is fixed by the court, either as a stated sum or on taxation. It covers only a contribution towards costs properly incurred in the action."},{t:"p",text:"Taxation is the process of assessing a bill item by item. It has costs and delay of its own, which is why parties often ask the court to fix a sum instead."},{t:"p",text:"The gap between the two bills is real, and it never closes entirely. Give the client that number before the case starts, not after it ends."}]},{id:"sec-l-costs-in-civil-litigation-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Conduct, the issues actually won, and offers to settle all pull the award away from the simple rule.",h:"What moves the figure",body:[{t:"p",text:"Costs follow the event is a starting point rather than a formula. Several things move it."},{t:"list",items:["Conduct. A hopeless point run to the last day, or correspondence left unanswered, can cost a winner part of the award.","Issues. A party who wins overall but loses a substantial issue may not recover the costs of that issue.","Offers. A claimant who turns down an offer and then does no better may bear the costs incurred after it was made.","Interlocutory applications. Each one carries its own costs order, and those accumulate long before any trial.","Costs thrown away, where a hearing is wasted because one side turned up unready."]},{t:"p",text:"A costs order is a real order. It is enforceable in the same ways as any other money judgment."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A plaintiff wins RM8,000 on one of five pleaded claims, and loses the other four after a long trial spent mostly on them. What costs order is likely?",options:["Something less than full costs, because he lost the issues the trial was actually spent on","Full costs, because costs follow the event and he is the successful party","No order as to costs, since each side won something"],answer:0,why:"He is the successful party, so the starting point favours him. But the court looks at what the trial was actually spent on, and four of the five claims failed. Expect an award reduced by reference to the issues, or even a separate order against him on the claims he lost."}]},{id:"sec-l-costs-in-civil-litigation-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Most cases settle because of what losing would cost, not because anyone changed their mind on the merits.",h:"How the risk shapes behaviour",body:[{t:"p",text:"Costs are the strongest pressure in civil litigation, and they work long before any judgment is delivered."},{t:"list",items:["A defendant weighs the claim, plus the claimant's costs, plus its own. That total is often several times the sum in dispute.","A claimant of modest means faces a bill it cannot pay if it loses, which deters good claims as well as bad ones.","A defendant may apply for security for costs where there is reason to doubt that a plaintiff could pay an award against it."]},{t:"caution",text:"Security for costs is not available against every plaintiff who looks poor. The grounds are set out in the Rules and they are limited, so read them before you threaten an application."},{t:"p",text:"An advice on the merits that says nothing about costs exposure is half an advice. Give the client both numbers."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-parties-and-joinder",moduleId:"m11-civil-procedure",title:"Who is on the record, and why it matters",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"A good claim can be lost at the start by naming the wrong person as defendant. This lesson covers who can sue and be sued, how parties are joined or changed, and what happens when the mistake is found after time has run.",source:"Rules of Court 2012 — the provisions on parties, on joinder, on amendment and substitution of parties, and on representative and third party proceedings. Limitation Act 1953. Companies Act 2016 on the effect of striking a company off the register.",verify:"This lesson states no Order or rule number for parties on purpose; confirm it in the current Rules of Court 2012 and record the date you checked. The same goes for joinder, amendment, representative actions and third party proceedings. No limitation period is stated either. Read the current Limitation Act 1953, and check whether another statute sets a special period for the claim in hand.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"rules-of-court-2012",where:"the orders on parties and on third party proceedings — read who may be joined, and when a party may be added or substituted"},{bookId:"janab-civil-procedure",where:"the chapters on parties and on amendment of pleadings and parties"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-parties-and-joinder-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Only a person the law recognises can sue or be sued, and a trading name is often not one.",h:"Suing a signboard",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Azman is hurt when scaffolding gives way at a site in Klang. The signboard on the hoarding says Sri Maju Construction."},{t:"p",text:"He sues that name. It turns out to be a business name registered to one man, and the site was actually run by a company nobody had mentioned."},{t:"p",text:"Naming the defendant is not clerical work. Get it wrong and there is nobody on the other side of the case."},{t:"rule",text:"A civil action must be brought by and against a person the law recognises: a natural person, a body corporate, or an entity which a statute or the Rules of Court 2012 permit to sue or be sued in a particular name.",source:"Rules of Court 2012"},{t:"list",items:["A sole proprietorship is not a person. The defendant is the owner, and the trading name may be added to identify the business.","A partnership may generally be sued in the firm's name, but the partners remain personally liable on the judgment.","A company is a separate person, and a director is not a defendant merely for being a director.","A dead person cannot be sued. The claim lies against the estate, through the personal representatives.","A club or society that was never incorporated has no legal personality, so the right defendants are usually its officers."]},{t:"predict",prompt:"Azman's lawyer then finds that the company which ran the site was struck off the register last year. What follows?",options:["The claim can proceed, because a struck-off company is still a defendant","The claim must be brought against its directors personally instead","There is no company to sue until it is restored to the register, and restoration is a separate application"],answer:2,reveal:"The third. A company that is off the register is not there to be sued, and a writ naming it is a writ against nobody. Restoring it takes its own application and its own time. That is why the identity of a corporate defendant is checked at the start, rather than after the limitation clock has run."}]},{id:"sec-l-parties-and-joinder-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A common question and a common transaction are what let several parties share one action.",h:"Joinder: more than one on a side",body:[{t:"p",text:"Joinder means bringing more than one claimant, or more than one defendant, into the same action."},{t:"p",text:"The test looks for a link. There must be a common question of law or fact, and rights arising out of the same transaction or series of transactions."},{t:"example",text:"Azman and two other workers hurt in the same collapse can sue together. A worker injured at a different site the year before cannot join them."},{t:"p",text:"The court may also add, substitute or strike out a party at any stage of the action, on application or of its own motion."},{t:"caution",text:"Joining every name on the site does not spread the risk. Defendants who should never have been sued get their costs, and it is your client who pays them."}]},{id:"sec-l-parties-and-joinder-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"An amendment that takes an accrued time bar away from a new defendant is the hardest one to obtain.",h:"Finding the right defendant too late",body:[{t:"p",text:"Azman learns the real employer's name only after the limitation period for his claim has run out."},{t:"p",text:"He asks to amend the writ and substitute the correct company. This is where two topics collide."},{t:"p",text:"Courts distinguish correcting a name from changing the defendant. The first is usually allowed. The second may not be."},{t:"table",caption:"Three ways the wrong name goes wrong",columns:["What happened","Usually fixable?","The catch"],rows:[["The right entity, misspelt or given its trading name","Yes","That defendant knew the claim was aimed at it, so nothing is taken away."],["The wrong entity altogether, found while time still runs","Yes, by adding or substituting","Expect to pay the costs of the party you wrongly named."],["The wrong entity altogether, found after time has run","Often not","Substitution would strip the new defendant of a limitation defence it has already acquired."]],alt:"Three situations. The right entity named with a misspelling, or by its trading name, is usually fixable, because that defendant knew the claim was aimed at it. The wrong entity found while time still runs is fixable by adding or substituting, though you will usually pay the wrongly named party's costs. The wrong entity found after time has run is often not fixable, because substitution would strip the new defendant of a limitation defence it has already acquired."},{t:"caution",text:"Limitation is a defence a defendant must plead, and a defendant added late will plead it. Check the identity of every defendant well before the clock gets close."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A writ names Sri Maju Construction Sdn Bhd. The correct name is Sri Maju Constructions Sdn Bhd, the same company, and the slip is spotted a month after time expired. What is the best argument?",options:["That limitation has no application to amendments at all","That the company waived the point by not correcting the writ itself","That this is a misnomer, because the intended defendant was always identifiable and knew the claim was aimed at it"],answer:2,why:"A misnomer corrects the label on a defendant who was always the intended target and knew it. Nothing is being taken away, because that company was the party sued from the beginning. The other two answers ask the court to ignore a limitation defence, rather than to show that none ever arose."}]},{id:"sec-l-parties-and-joinder-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"One claim can stand for many with the same interest, and a defendant can pull in whoever should indemnify him.",h:"Representative actions, and bringing in a third party",body:[{t:"p",text:"Two devices deal with numbers, one on each side of the record."},{t:"p",text:"A representative action lets one or more people sue, or be sued, on behalf of others who share the same interest in the claim."},{t:"caution",text:"Same interest is a real requirement rather than a formality. Malaysia has no general class action procedure, and claims where the loss differs from person to person usually cannot be run this way."},{t:"p",text:"Third party proceedings work in the other direction. A defendant who says somebody else must indemnify him brings that person into the same action."},{t:"example",text:"The developer sued by Azman brings in the scaffolding subcontractor. Liability and indemnity are then decided once, instead of in two separate trials on the same facts."},{t:"p",text:"Both devices save the cost of running the same facts twice. Both have their own steps in the Rules, so build the time into the timetable."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-interlocutory",moduleId:"m11-civil-procedure",title:"Ending a case early, and holding the position meanwhile",minutes:30,summary:"Most civil cases never reach trial. Order 14, Order 18 rule 19 and Order 29 of the Rules of Court 2012 are how they end, and each has a different threshold.",plants:["ms-civ-01","ms-civ-02","ms-civ-03","ms-civ-04"],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"janab-civil-procedure",where:"the chapters on summary judgment, striking out and injunctions"},{bookId:"malaysian-civil-procedure-white-book",where:"for the rules themselves with commentary"}],source:"Rules of Court 2012, Orders 14, 18 r 19 and 29; Limitation Act 1953 s 6; American Cyanamid Co v Ethicon Ltd [1975] AC 396; Keet Gerald Francis Noel John v Mohd Noor bin Abdullah [1995] 1 MLJ 193 (CA).",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Check the Rules of Court 2012 for amendments. Keet Gerald is the Malaysian decision to cite for the interlocutory injunction test, and its citation should be confirmed in a report you can hold. The local gloss on American Cyanamid is stated differently in different judgments, so check for later Federal Court authority before relying on the formulation above.",plate:"halt",plateCaption:"A hand on the works. Interim relief holds the position until trial; it does not decide who is right.",readMinutes:7,sections:[{id:"sec-l-interlocutory-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Order 14 asks whether there is a triable issue, not whether the defence would win.",anchor:"fig-l-interlocutory-0",h:"Summary judgment: Order 14",body:[{t:"p",text:"A supplier is owed RM60,000 on invoices the buyer signed for. The buyer files a defence saying only that he disputes the sum. Must the supplier wait two years for a trial?"},{t:"p",text:"Not necessarily. A plaintiff who says the defence is hopeless may apply for judgment without a trial. That is Order 14."},{t:"p",text:"The application is supported by affidavit — a written statement sworn to be true. It verifies the facts and states a belief that there is no defence."},{t:"predict",prompt:"The buyer's defence says only that he disputes the sum. What does he have to show to stop judgment being entered now, without a trial?",options:["That his defence will probably succeed at trial","That there is an issue in dispute which ought to be tried","That the supplier cannot prove the debt"],answer:1,reveal:"He only has to show there is something worth trying. The court is not asking who would win. A defence that is thin but not hopeless may still get him in, on conditions such as paying money into court. If you expected him to show a good defence, you are in good company. Weighing the merits as though at trial is the standard way to get this wrong."},{t:"rule",text:"The defendant resists by showing an issue or question in dispute which ought to be tried, or that there ought for some other reason to be a trial. Where he does, unconditional leave to defend follows; where the defence is shadowy, leave may be conditional.",source:"Rules of Court 2012, Order 14"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: the defendant does not have to win. He has to show there is something worth trying. A defence that is thin but not hopeless may get him in on conditions, such as paying money into court."},{t:"caution",text:"The threshold is not whether the defence will succeed. It is whether there is a triable issue, and an answer that weighs the merits as if at trial has applied the wrong test."},{t:"compare",title:"Two applications that look alike and are not",left:{h:"Summary judgment — O 14",items:["Says: the defence has no substance **on the evidence**.","Affidavits go in on both sides.","Threshold: is there a triable issue? Not, would the defence win.","A shadowy defence may get conditional leave to defend."]},right:{h:"Striking out — O 18 r 19",items:["Says: the pleading is bad **as a document**.","On the no-reasonable-cause-of-action limb, no evidence is admissible at all.","The pleaded facts are assumed true.","Used sparingly, and only in plain and obvious cases."]}}]},{id:"sec-l-interlocutory-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Order 18 rule 19 attacks the pleading as a document, and on one limb no evidence is admissible at all.",h:"Striking out: Order 18 rule 19",body:[{t:"p",text:"Sometimes the problem is not the evidence. It is the document itself."},{t:"p",text:"A pleading is the document setting out a party's case in material facts — the statement of claim, or the defence."},{t:"list",items:["The court may strike out a pleading which discloses no reasonable cause of action or defence.","It may also strike out one that is scandalous, frivolous or vexatious, or that may prejudice, embarrass or delay the fair trial, or that is otherwise an abuse of process.","On the first ground no evidence is admissible. The question is taken on the pleading as it stands.","The jurisdiction is used sparingly, and only in plain and obvious cases. A difficult point of law is not by itself a reason to strike out."]},{t:"p",text:"The distinction from Order 14 is worth stating. Order 14 says the defence has no substance on the evidence. Order 18 rule 19 says the pleading is bad as a document."}]},{id:"sec-l-interlocutory-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Serious question, damages inadequate, balance of convenience — plus an undertaking in damages.",h:"Interlocutory injunctions: Order 29",body:[{t:"p",text:"A landlord starts clearing a tenant's stock out of a shoplot while the dispute over the tenancy is still running. By the time a trial comes, the stock will be gone."},{t:"p",text:"An injunction is a court order to do something, or to stop doing something. An interlocutory injunction is one that holds the position until trial."},{t:"rule",text:"The applicant must show a serious question to be tried; that damages would not be an adequate remedy; and that the balance of convenience lies in favour of granting the injunction. An undertaking in damages is ordinarily required.",source:"Rules of Court 2012, Order 29; Keet Gerald Francis Noel John v Mohd Noor bin Abdullah [1995] 1 MLJ 193 (CA), applying American Cyanamid Co v Ethicon Ltd [1975] AC 396"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: show there is a real dispute worth trying. Show that money paid afterwards would not put it right. Show that holding the position does less harm than not holding it."},{t:"p",text:"The undertaking in damages is a promise to compensate the other side if the order turns out to have been wrongly granted. It is not a formality."},{t:"p",text:"The Malaysian courts have their own gloss on this. The case to cite is *Keet Gerald Francis Noel John v Mohd Noor bin Abdullah* [1995] 1 MLJ 193, a Court of Appeal decision."},{t:"p",text:"It asks the judge to find a *bona fide* serious question to be tried, rather than merely a question that is not frivolous. The judge does not weigh the merits at this stage. He then asks where the justice of the case lies, setting the harm an injunction would do against the harm of refusing it."},{t:"p",text:"An *ex parte* application is one made without notice to the other side. There the duty of full and frank disclosure is strict, and a breach of it is itself a ground for discharge."},{t:"caution",text:"A mandatory interlocutory injunction — one requiring something to be done rather than restrained — attracts a higher threshold. Identify which kind you are asking for before you state the test."}]},{id:"sec-l-interlocutory-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The strongest claim is worth nothing brought too late, and limitation must be pleaded.",anchor:"fig-l-interlocutory-1",h:"Limitation, which decides more cases than any of these",body:[{t:"p",text:"The strongest claim in the world is worth nothing if it is brought too late."},{t:"p",text:"The Limitation Act 1953 sets six years for actions founded on contract or on tort. Time runs from the date on which the cause of action accrued."},{t:"p",text:"Different periods apply to actions to recover land and to certain other claims. There are also provisions on disability, on acknowledgment, and on part payment."},{t:"caution",text:"Limitation is a defence that must be pleaded, and a court will not take the point for a defendant who has not. Check the current text of the Act for the period applicable to the particular claim. Check also whether a special limitation provision in another statute displaces it."},{t:"chart",kind:"bar",title:"Limitation periods, by kind of claim",data:[{label:"Contract",value:6},{label:"Tort",value:6,note:"generally runs from when damage occurs, not from the act"},{label:"Recovery of land",value:12},{label:"Enforcing a judgment",value:12}],unit:"years",series:[{key:"value",label:"Years"}],caption:"Years from the accrual of the cause of action. Accrual is not the same as the wrongful act — in contract it runs from breach, in negligence generally from damage.",source:"Limitation Act 1953. Check the current text for the period applicable to the particular claim.",note:"Other statutes contain their own, shorter periods, and limitation is a defence that must be pleaded — a court will not take the point for a defendant who has not."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A contract claim is filed eight years after the breach. The defendant's lawyer says nothing about the delay. What should the court do?",options:["Refuse the claim; the period for a contract claim is six years","Ask the defendant whether he wants to rely on limitation","Hear it — limitation is a defence and it has to be pleaded"],answer:2,why:"The claim is out of time. But the time bar is a defence, and the court will not take the point for a defendant who has not raised it. The first answer treats limitation as something the court polices by itself. Check as well whether another statute sets a special period."}]},{id:"sec-l-interlocutory-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Judgment is a right to be paid; enforcement is a separate exercise with its own procedures.",h:"Judgment is not money",body:[{t:"p",text:"Winning is one step. Getting paid is another one."},{t:"p",text:"Enforcement is a separate exercise with its own procedures. There is a writ of seizure and sale, and there are garnishee proceedings, which capture money that others owe the debtor. There is a judgment debtor summons, and a charging order. And there is winding up or bankruptcy, where the debtor simply cannot pay."},{t:"p",text:"An advice that stops at 'the plaintiff will obtain judgment' has stopped one step early."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qil1",q:"What must a defendant show to resist summary judgment under Order 14?",options:["A triable issue, or some other reason for a trial","That the defence will succeed","That the plaintiff acted in bad faith","That the claim is time-barred"],answer:0,why:"A triable issue, or some other reason for a trial. Not that the defence will actually succeed.",source:"Rules of Court 2012 O 14"},{id:"qil2",q:"On an application to strike out for disclosing no reasonable cause of action, what evidence is admissible?",options:["Affidavit evidence from both sides","The documents only","Oral evidence","None — the pleading is taken as it stands"],answer:3,why:"Under that limb of O 18 r 19 the question is decided on the pleading alone.",source:"Rules of Court 2012, Order 18 rule 19"},{id:"qil3",q:"Which of these is *not* part of the interlocutory injunction test?",options:["Adequacy of damages","A serious question to be tried","Whether the applicant will probably win at trial","Balance of convenience"],answer:2,why:"The court does not try the merits at the interlocutory stage. It holds the position until trial.",source:"Keet Gerald Francis Noel John v Mohd Noor bin Abdullah [1995] 1 MLJ 193 (CA); American Cyanamid Co v Ethicon Ltd [1975] AC 396"},{id:"qil4",q:"What is the special obligation on an ex parte application?",options:["A larger undertaking in damages","Service on the defendant first","Leave of a judge in chambers","Full and frank disclosure"],answer:3,why:"Full and frank disclosure. Breaking it is itself a ground for discharging the order.",source:"Rules of Court 2012, Order 29; the duty of full and frank disclosure on an ex parte application"},{id:"qil5",q:"What is the general limitation period for an action founded on contract or tort?",options:["Six years from accrual","Three years","Twelve years","Six years from discovery"],answer:0,why:"Six years from accrual, subject to the special periods and to any statute that displaces the Act.",source:"Limitation Act 1953 s 6"},{id:"qil6",q:"Why is 'the plaintiff will obtain judgment' an incomplete advice?",options:["Enforcement is a separate exercise","Judgments are not final","Appeals are automatic","Costs are not included"],answer:0,why:"A judgment is a right to be paid, not payment. The enforcement route has to be advised on too.",source:"Rules of Court 2012; enforcement is a separate exercise from obtaining judgment"}]}];export{e as default};
